items.body:
I've noticed a few cases of breakage near what some would say are reasonable limits with pedestrian isochrones (they may be present with other modes but I haven't observed them). The problem seems to be related to (relatively) high pedestrian speeds.

For example:

```json
{
  "locations": [
    {"lat": 51.53532, "lon": -0.13812}
  ],
  "costing": "pedestrian",
  "contours": [ { "time": 90.0 } ],
  "polygons": true,
  "costing_options": {
    "pedestrian": { "walking_speed": 12 }
  }
}
```

This returns the shape of [a reservoir](https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/23129884#map=15/51.62949/-0.02309). Reducing the speed to 11 gives a bizarre slice of the Thames many miles south 😅 

<img width="996" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bbb5d753-b221-45c5-a754-9ca7219e0117" />

**It seems like something is going on related to simplification and/or how various rings are build / interpreted. _Generating linestrings instead of polygons creates non-closed linestrings, often in different locations_.**

Reducing the walking speed to 10 in this case produces a reasonable shape.

I have not yet found an example in which increasing the speed a bit further "un-breaks" the result (just an observation; not saying anything definitive). Typically, increasing speed further yields a different broken shape in some part of the area that I'd expect the isochrone to cover.

Here's another example in London with a 15 minute contour:

```json
{
  "locations": [
    {"lat": 51.513156, "lon": -0.11579}
  ],
  "costing": "pedestrian",
  "contours": [
    {"time": 15.0}
  ],
  "polygons": true,
  "costing_options": {
    "pedestrian": {
      "walking_speed": 15.0
    }
  }
}
```

The geometry breaks and reduces to a small section of the Thames 😅 Reducing `walking_speed` to 14 works.

<img width="949" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/01a3adb3-76bb-4dae-a98b-fa7dffa59082" />

The examples I've provided are in London, but it's not exclusive to there.

# Possibly related sub-issue?

I'm not sure if this is related, but it's also possible to trigger a C++ exception due to an unexpected code path. Adding denoise to the first request...

```json
{
  "locations": [
    {"lat": 51.53532, "lon": -0.13812}
  ],
  "costing": "pedestrian",
  "contours": [ { "time": 90.0 } ],
  "polygons": true,
  "denoise": 0.5,
  "costing_options": {
    "pedestrian": { "walking_speed": 12 }
  }
}
```

... yields the following response:

```json
{
  "error_code": 499,
  "error": "Unknown: vector::_M_range_check: __n (which is 0) >= this->size() (which is 0)",
  "status_code": 400,
  "status": "Bad Request"
}
```

The log includes the following message one or more times in such cases:

```
[WARN] No exterior ring contour found for inner contour.
```

The _immediate_ cause of that exception appears to be out of bounds access when the size of the geometry is zero on this line: https://github.com/valhalla/valhalla/blob/868cb5dc770c14a96a2a976da13d9e0c093beb48/src/tyr/isochrone_serializer.cc#L290.

"Fixing" this with a bounds check around `features->emplace_back` yields zero features for this example. We should probably do a bounds check here, but at the moment, that just masks a deeper issue with an empty response.

I'm not sure if the denoise problem and the broken geometries from "high" speeds are related, but it was the second issue that led me to discover the first, and both seem to be a result of suspect geometry handling.

Anyone have any ideas what else to look at for debugging this?
